      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-185289",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following statement:\n\n> Given that $A$, $B$, and $C$ are sets, $A \\cdot (B\u222aC) = (A \\cdot B)\u222a(A \\cdot C)$.\n\nA proof of this statement is given below.\n\n> $L1$: Let $(x, y) \u2208 A \\cdot (B\u222aC)$, which is equivalent to the following:\n> $L2$: $(x \u2208 A)\u2227(y \u2208 B\u222aC)$\n> $L3$: $(x \u2208 A)\u2227(y \u2208 B\u2228y \u2208 C)$\n> $L4$: $(x \u2208 A\u2227y \u2208 B)\u2228(x \u2208 A\u2227y \u2208 C)$\n> $L5$: $(x, y) \u2208 A \\cdot B\u2228(x, y) \u2208 A \\cdot C$\n> $L6$: $(x, y) \u2208 (A \\cdot B)\u222a(A \\cdot C)$\n> $L7$: Thus, $A \\cdot (B\u222aC) = (A \\cdot B)\u222a(A \\cdot C)$.\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from line $L1$ by the definition of {{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from line $L2$ by the definition of {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from line $L3$ by {{field-3}}."
    },
